    "body": "A view of the Zgounder pit. Credit: Aya Gold & Silver.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 12, 2026\nAya Gold & Silver (TSX: AYA; Nasdaq: AYA) led the silver ranking with a shallow, high-grade intercept at its Zgounder mine in Morocco. Hole DZG-SF-26-945 cut 3.5 metres grading 3,501 grams silver per tonne from 16 metres depth.\nThe result was part of a Sept. 16 update on drilling near the pit and at depth. The Aya Gold & Silver program tested for extensions around the producing mine, where additional drilling can help establish whether mineralization continues beyond the areas already defined.\nThe next two results show how different intervals can finish close together in the ranking. At Mineral Point, part of the Ruby Hill project in Nevada, i-80 Gold (TSX: IAU; NYSE-A: IAUX) returned 170.7 metres at 61.5 grams silver. Cambria Gold Mines (TSXV: CAMB) came in just behind it with 3.1 metres at 3,382.7 grams at Premier in British Columbia. The Nevada interval was more than 50 times as long, while the Premier interval was about 55 times as rich.\nThe chart ranks silver assays reported from Sept. 16 to Oct. 15, 2026, by grade multiplied by reported interval length. It shows the highest-scoring hole per property. Reported lengths may differ from true widths.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Silver\nCOMPANY: Aya Gold & Silver Cambria Gold Mines i-80 Gold\nRepublish this article",
